  /// mode allows (offline it waits for [`EpicWallet::contacts_outbox_deliver`]). The contact's
  /// answer comes back by collecting, which finalizes and posts the payment and raises
  /// `ContactPaymentFinalized`. `purpose` defaults to a payment. A contact that never answers
  /// leaves the payment `Sent`: `cancel_transaction` gives it up and unlocks the inputs; if
  /// the contact's answer arrives after that, it is refused.
  ///
  /// Fails, with nothing locked, with `ContactNotFound` for a contact that is not paired or
  /// whose keys changed, `SealingOff` when its wallet cannot open sealed slates, and as
  /// `create_send_slate` does. A send that could not be queued is cancelled before the error
  /// returns.
  Future<SentTx> contactSend({
    required String contactId,
    required BigInt amount,
    required BigInt minimumConfirmations,
    String? message,
    SendOptions? options,
    SlatePurpose? purpose,
    required ContactRelayMode relayMode,
  });

  /// "Allow invoices" on or off for a contact. Turning it off declines the contact's unpaid
  /// invoices, queuing a `declined` for each in the outbox; a paid one finishes.
  ///
  /// Fails with `ContactNotVerified` when turning it on for a contact whose words were not
  /// checked, and `ContactNotFound`.
  Future<void> contactSetAllowInvoices({
    required String contactId,
    required bool allow,
  });

  /// Sets the user's own name for a contact; an empty label clears it. A label follows the
  /// rule of card names: at most 48 bytes, no control or hidden formatting characters. Fails
  /// with `InvalidContactName` for another label and with `ContactNotFound`.
  Future<void> contactSetLabel({
    required String contactId,
    required String label,
  });

  /// "Seal payments" on or off for a contact. Fails with `ContactNotFound`.
  Future<void> contactSetSeal({required String contactId, required bool seal});

  /// "Remove pairing" that tells the contact: as [`EpicWallet::contact_remove`], after a
  /// signed unpaired notice to the contact's mailbox, best effort and waiting at most a few
  /// seconds for its relay, so the contact sees `ContactUnpaired` instead of sending to a
  /// pairing nobody reads. Nothing is sent with `relay_mode` Offline or Epicbox off, for a
  /// pairing in progress, or for one the contact removed first. Returns whether anything
  /// was removed.
  Future<bool> contactUnpair({
    required String contactId,
    required ContactRelayMode relayMode,
  });

  /// The paired contacts, oldest first. Pairings in progress are in
  /// [`EpicWallet::pairings_pending`].
  Future<List<ContactView>> contacts();

  /// Collects the pairing and contact mailboxes once and raises an event for what arrived:
  /// replies and confirmations pair, contacts' payments and withdrawals are received and
  /// answered through the outbox, their invoices wait in [`EpicWallet::contact_invoices`].
  /// Pairings past their time end with `PairingFailed`. A relay that does not answer is listed
  /// in `unreachable`, raises `HomeRelayUnreachable` and is tried again later, not returned as
  /// an error. Messages are taken off a relay only once their effect is stored, so a call
  /// cut short loses nothing.
  ///
  /// With `only`, just the mailboxes of those contact ids are drained, their pairings in
  /// progress included: a cheap look at a few pairings that leaves the others' turn as it
  /// was. The outbox and the expiry of pairings run as in a full collect.
  ///
  /// Fails with `ContactsOffline` or `EpicboxOff` before connecting, and with
  /// `InvalidArgument` for an id that is not one or more than 64 of them.
  Future<ContactCollectReport> contactsCollect({
    required CollectTrigger trigger,
    required ContactRelayMode relayMode,
    List<String>? only,
  });

  /// The pairings as a versioned JSON document for the app's `.wfeh` history backup. It
  /// holds no secret: the keys come from the recovery phrase. Pairings in progress are left
  /// out.
  Future<String> contactsExport();

  /// Restores the pairings of a document [`EpicWallet::contacts_export`] wrote, with the
  /// same keys, mailboxes and trust, marked as possibly living on another device too.
  /// Pairings this wallet holds already are kept as they are.
  ///
  /// Fails with `InvalidArgument` for a document over 32 MiB, and with `InvalidArgument` or
  /// `Io` for a document this build cannot read or that is for the other network; nothing is
  /// imported then. A pairing whose keys this wallet's seed does not derive is skipped as a
  /// conflict.
  Future<ContactImportSummary> contactsImport({required String document});

  /// Moves this wallet's home relay for its contacts to `relay` (`domain[:port]`): every
  /// paired contact that has not ended gets this wallet's card naming its mailbox there,
  /// once, as a contact update. Returns how many contacts were told. A contact that takes
  /// the update sees `ContactRelayMoveRequested` and follows once the app on its side
  /// applies it. For `drain_days` (0 to 365) every collect still reads the old mailboxes:
  /// what a contact sends there is taken, and a `contact_check` there is answered with the
  /// card naming the new mailbox, so a contact away longer than the relay keeps the update
  /// still learns of the move. The drain takes the one previous-home slot a contact has,
  /// replacing one left by following that contact's move. With 0 the old mailboxes are not
  /// read again: a contact that never takes the update stops reaching this wallet and has
  /// to pair again, which `contact_check` shows as no answer. Private pairings and pairings
  /// in progress keep their relay. Switch the wallet's Epicbox relay to `relay` too, so new
  /// invites name it.
  ///
  /// Fails with `InvalidArgument` for a host that is not a relay's name, or a drain longer
  /// than 365 days.
  Future<int> contactsMoveHome({required String relay, required int drainDays});

  /// Posts every outbox entry that is due, raising `OutboxDelivered` for each that reached
  /// its relay and `OutboxStuck` for one undelivered for a day, which stops retrying. A relay
  /// that fails is counted, not returned.
  ///
  /// Fails with `ContactsOffline` or `EpicboxOff` before connecting.
  Future<OutboxReport> contactsOutboxDeliver({
    required ContactRelayMode relayMode,
  });

  /// Gives every contact pairing that has not ended this wallet's card again, naming it
  /// `name`, as a contact update the next collect posts; private pairings are left alone.
  /// Returns how many were queued. Call it with `pairing_standing_invite(name)` when the
  /// user changes the name contacts see. Fails with `InvalidContactName`.
  Future<int> contactsSetOwnName({required String name});

  /// Creates a wallet in `config.data_dir` with its vault in `store`. With `mnemonic` it
  /// restores that seed (call [`EpicWallet::scan`] next); without it a fresh seed is
  /// generated, whose phrase [`EpicWallet::mnemonic`] returns for the backup screen. Returns
  /// the open wallet.
  ///
  /// `restore_from_height` is the height the restore scan starts from: a scan from it or below
  /// completes the restore, and outputs below the height that the chain still holds are not
  /// found by it. `None` and 0 start from genesis. Pass a height only when the phrase was
  /// first used above it.
  ///
  /// Fails with `InvalidArgument` when
  /// `restore_from_height` comes without `mnemonic`, before anything is written,
  /// `WalletAlreadyExists` when `store` holds a vault, `InvalidMnemonic`,
  /// `ForeignWallet` when the directory holds another wallet's database, `WalletBusy` when a
  /// wallet on the directory is open, and `SecretStore` when the vault could not be written.
  static Future<EpicWallet> create({
    required LepicRuntime runtime,
    required WalletConfig config,
    required AppSecretStore store,
    String? mnemonic,
    BigInt? restoreFromHeight,
    required String password,
  }) => RustLib.instance.api.crateApiWalletEpicWalletCreate(
    runtime: runtime,
    config: config,
    store: store,
    mnemonic: mnemonic,
    restoreFromHeight: restoreFromHeight,
    password: password,
  );

  /// Step 1 of a manual send: builds the slate, locks the inputs and returns the slate text
  /// for the recipient, whose answer goes to [`EpicWallet::finalize_and_post`].
  ///
  /// `message` goes to the recipient signed with the slate, as `epic-wallet send --message`
  /// sends it. It is cut to its first 256 bytes, on a character boundary, before it is signed.
  ///
  /// `options` chooses the inputs and change, `Smallest` with one change output when `None`;
  /// change outputs outside 1..=10 fail with `InvalidArgument`.
  Future<Slate> createSendSlate({
    required BigInt amount,
    required BigInt minimumConfirmations,
    String? message,
    SendOptions? options,
  });

  /// [`EpicWallet::create`] with a wallet password LEPIC generates, seals under `pin` and
  /// never hands to Dart. The sealed text is bound to a random key LEPIC writes into `store`
  /// as `lepic.pin-key`, so it opens only together with that store.
  ///
  /// Store `sealed_password` before showing the recovery phrase or receiving funds. If the
  /// app stops before storing it, the wallet cannot be opened by PIN; delete it with
  /// [`delete_wallet`] and create it again (a restored wallet from its phrase).
  ///
  /// `restore_from_height` is as in `create`. Fails with `InvalidArgument` when `pin` has
  /// fewer than `PinSettings.min_len` characters (6 by default), and otherwise as `create` does.
  static Future<PinWallet> createWithPin({
    required LepicRuntime runtime,
    required WalletConfig config,
    required AppSecretStore store,
    String? mnemonic,
    BigInt? restoreFromHeight,
    required String pin,
  }) => RustLib.instance.api.crateApiWalletEpicWalletCreateWithPin(
    runtime: runtime,
    config: config,
    store: store,
    mnemonic: mnemonic,
    restoreFromHeight: restoreFromHeight,
    pin: pin,
  );

  /// Signs a device-link `transcript` with this wallet's device-link key, an Ed25519 key of
  /// the phrase made for nothing else, under the tag `lepic/device-link/v1`, and returns the
  /// 64-byte signature. Every install of the phrase holds the same key, so
  /// [`EpicWallet::device_link_verify`] on the other device takes it. Needs no relay. Fails
  /// with `InvalidArgument` for a `transcript` over 64 KiB.
  Future<Uint8List> deviceLinkSign({required List<int> transcript});

  /// Whether `signature` is [`EpicWallet::device_link_sign`] of `transcript` by a wallet of
  /// this wallet's phrase. False for anything malformed and a `transcript` over 64 KiB.
  Future<bool> deviceLinkVerify({
    required List<int> transcript,
    required List<int> signature,
  });

  /// Connects to the relay once, handles every pending message and disconnects: the call for
  /// background work. While the listener runs it does nothing and reports nothing handled,
  /// as the listener receives the same messages.
  Future<InboxReport> drainEpicbox();

  /// Takes what waits at the listing address of [`EpicWallet::epicbox_address_at`], on its
  /// own relay connection, as [`EpicWallet::drain_epicbox`] does for the payments address,
  /// which stays undisturbed. A payment is received, answered from the listing address, and
  /// its transaction names the listing (`TransactionDetails.receive_listing`). One that asks
  /// for a payment proof is refused (`ForeignProofAddress` in the log) and nothing is signed
  /// for it. Later notices about such a payment go from nowhere else. Fails as
  /// `epicbox_address_at` and `drain_epicbox` do.
  Future<InboxReport> drainEpicboxAt({
    required String serviceKey,
    required int generation,
  });

  /// The epic1 address of the default account: its seal key and its epic1 proof key on the
  /// wallet's network.
  Future<String> epic1Address();

  /// The answers this wallet made for payments taken at its link mailboxes, with the relay
  /// each goes to and whether it was posted.
  Future<List<Epic1AnswerView>> epic1Answers({
    required ContactRelayMode relayMode,
  });

  /// Whether the listener `socket` takes payments sealed to the wallet's epic1 addresses from
  /// its next start, as [`EpicWallet::set_epic1_at_listener`] last saved it.
  bool epic1AtListener({required ListenerSocket socket});

  /// Makes this restored install the one that issues new numbered epic1 addresses. Call it
  /// once no other install on the same recovery phrase issues them: two installs that both
  /// issue hand the same address to two payers. Calling it again changes nothing.
  Future<void> epic1IssueHere();

  /// Whether this install issues new numbered epic1 addresses. A wallet created fresh does;
  /// one restored from its recovery phrase does once [`EpicWallet::epic1_issue_here`] made it
  /// the issuer. The address [`EpicWallet::epic1_address`] shows is there either way.
  Future<bool> epic1Issuing();

  /// Issues a link to the wallet's epic1 address, asking `amount` atomic units or, with
  /// `None`, any amount the payer chooses. The link names its mailbox on each of `relays`,
  /// `domain[:port]`, at most three, or on the wallet's own relay when `relays` is empty. It
  /// has no expiry. Collecting drains an on-screen link's mailbox for seven days after it was
  /// issued or last used, and a printed link's until it is retired.
  ///
  /// Fails with `InvalidArgument` for a zero amount, `PrintedLinksFull` when all 32 printed
  /// links are in use, `Epic1RelayInvalid` for more than three relays or one that is not a
  /// domain with an optional port or is on this device, `OwnRelayLocal` when the wallet's own
  /// relay is on this device, and `Epic1MessageInvalid` for a message longer than a link
  /// carries or with control characters.
  Future<Epic1Link> epic1LinkMake({
    BigInt? amount,
    String? message,
    required Epic1LinkKind kind,
    required List<String> relays,
    required ContactRelayMode relayMode,
  });

  /// Reads an epic1 link: the address it pays, what it asks, and the relays a payment would
  /// be posted to, to show before the user pays. Nothing is stored.
  ///
  /// Fails with `NotAnEpic1Link` for text that is not one, `Epic1WrongNetwork` for a link of
  /// the other network, and `Epic1CannotPay` when it names no relay this wallet can use.
  Future<Epic1LinkRead> epic1LinkRead({
    required String link,
    required ContactRelayMode relayMode,
  });

  /// Frees the printed link `n`: its mailbox is no longer drained and the next printed link
  /// may take it. Fails with `Epic1NotFound` when no printed link `n` is in use.
  Future<void> epic1LinkRetire({required int n});

  /// The link mailboxes of the default account in use, by kind and number.
  Future<List<Epic1LinkView>> epic1Links();

  /// Pays an epic1 link `amount` atomic units, or with `None` the amount the link asks. The payment names a reply mailbox on the wallet's own relay, is stored, then posted
  /// to the link's first relay, and to the next when a post fails, at most three.
  /// `hosts_ok` says the user confirmed every relay [`EpicWallet::epic1_link_read`] showed.
  /// Collecting takes the answer and finalizes it.
  ///
  /// Fails as [`EpicWallet::epic1_link_read`] does, with `InvalidArgument` for a zero amount
  /// or none when the link asks none, `RelayNotConfirmed` when `hosts_ok` is false,
  /// `OwnRelayLocal` when the wallet's own relay is on this device, `Epic1PaymentPending`
  /// while an earlier payment to the link waits for its answer, and as `send` fails.
  Future<Epic1Paid> epic1Pay({
    required String link,
    BigInt? amount,
    required BigInt minimumConfirmations,
    String? message,
    SendOptions? options,
    required bool hostsOk,
    required ContactRelayMode relayMode,
  });

  /// Posts the answer for the payment `slate_id` to the reply mailbox the payer named.
  /// `relay_ok` says the user confirmed the relay [`EpicWallet::epic1_answers`] showed.
  /// Posting again is safe.
  ///
  /// Fails with `RelayNotConfirmed` when `relay_ok` is false, `Epic1NotFound` when no answer
  /// is stored, and `TransportFailed` when the post failed.
  ///
  /// Returns the format the answer went out in, always `Native`.
  Future<SlateWire> epic1PostAnswer({
    required String slateId,
    required bool relayOk,
    required ContactRelayMode relayMode,
  });

  /// Posts the stored payment `slate_id` again, the same bytes, to the relays of its link
  /// after the one it was last posted to. `hosts_ok` says the user confirmed them.
  ///
  /// Fails with `RelayNotConfirmed` when `hosts_ok` is false, `Epic1NotFound` when no
  /// payment is stored, `Epic1Finalized` once it was finalized, and `Epic1NoMoreRelays` when
  /// every relay was tried.
  Future<Epic1Paid> epic1PostNext({
    required String slateId,
    required bool hostsOk,
    required ContactRelayMode relayMode,
  });

  /// After a restore from the phrase: drains, once, all 50 on-screen and all 32 printed link
  /// mailboxes on the wallet's own relay, and the reply mailboxes of the pending payments.
  /// A link mailbox that held a payment is marked in use; links named on other relays are not
  /// found. Returns how many payments were taken and answers finalized.
  ///
  /// Fails with `TransportFailed` when a drain failed.
  Future<int> epic1Restore({required ContactRelayMode relayMode});

  /// The wallet's Epicbox address, `<key>@<domain>[:port]`. Fails with `Unsupported` when
  /// the configuration names no relay.
  Future<String> epicboxAddress();

  /// The Epicbox address `<key>@<relay>[:port]` of this wallet's listing with the name
  /// service whose key is `service_key` (a compressed secp256k1 key, 66 hex digits), generation
  /// `generation`, on the wallet's relay. Its key comes from the phrase alone, so every
  /// install of the phrase and a restore give the same address; raise `generation` after a
  /// leave for a new one. Needs no relay. Fails with `InvalidArgument` for another `service_key`
  /// and with `Unsupported` when the configuration names no relay.
  Future<String> epicboxAddressAt({
    required String serviceKey,
    required int generation,
  });

  /// Whether Epicbox over Tor is on, as [`EpicWallet::set_epicbox_over_tor`] set it.
  bool epicboxOverTor();

  /// Opens sealed text, `BEGINEPICTX.` or `BEGINEPICSEALED.`, with this wallet's keys and
  /// shows its slate as it arrived, as JSON. Nothing is stored and no contact is refreshed.
  ///
  /// Fails as [`EpicWallet::sealed_open`] does, with `SavedTransaction` for a record another
  /// Epic wallet keeps in its `saved_txs` directory.
  Future<EpictxView> epictxOpen({required String text});

  /// The fee and inputs a send of `amount` with `minimum_confirmations` would take, chosen
  /// as [`EpicWallet::create_send_slate`], [`EpicWallet::send_file`] and
  /// [`EpicWallet::send_epicbox`] choose them, with nothing locked. Such a send at the same
  /// chain tip, with no outputs changed in between, charges this fee. Asks the node for its
  /// tip. Fails as those sends do, for example with `NotEnoughFunds` or `NodeUnreachable`.
  /// Pass the `options` the send will get.
  ///
  /// `options` chooses the inputs and change, `Smallest` with one change output when `None`;
  /// change outputs outside 1..=10 fail with `InvalidArgument`.
  Future<FeeEstimate> estimateFee({
    required BigInt amount,
    required BigInt minimumConfirmations,
    SendOptions? options,
  });

  /// The largest amount a send with `minimum_confirmations` can pay now, with its fee and
  /// inputs. A send of that amount at the same chain tip, with no outputs changed in between,
  /// charges this fee. Fails with `NotEnoughFunds` when no amount can be paid, and otherwise
  /// as [`EpicWallet::estimate_fee`]. Sending everything takes `All`: with `Smallest`
  /// the largest amount is the one the smallest outputs that cover it can pay, which is the
  /// same total here but may differ past 500 outputs.
  ///
  /// `options` chooses the inputs and change, `Smallest` with one change output when `None`;
  /// change outputs outside 1..=10 fail with `InvalidArgument`.
  Future<FeeEstimate> estimateMax({
    required BigInt minimumConfirmations,
    SendOptions? options,
  });

  /// The inputs, outputs and fee of [`EpicWallet::send_to_self`] with `request` now, with
  /// nothing locked: `amount` is the first output and `change` the others. A send to self of
  /// the same request at the same chain tip, with no outputs changed in between, charges this
  /// fee. Fails as that call does before it stores anything.
  Future<FeeEstimate> estimateSendToSelf({
    required SelfSendRequest request,
    required BigInt minimumConfirmations,
  });

  /// Everything the wallet reports from now on: session, balance, transaction, sync and
  /// transport changes. The stream ends when the wallet is dropped. A failure to subscribe,
  /// which only a defect inside LEPIC causes, ends the stream with a `Panic` [`WalletError`]
  /// as its error.
  Stream<WalletEvent> events();

  void expectTor();

  /// Sender or issuer side: finalizes the answer and posts the transaction to the node. The
  /// answer is the recipient's S2 of a send this wallet made, or the payer's I2 of an invoice
  /// this wallet issued; the stage decides. An I2 finalizes only an invoice this wallet
  /// issued and still waits for, for the amount and outputs it asked: `InvalidSlate` for a
  /// slate id it did not issue or a signature that does not verify, `InvoiceChanged` for a
  /// payment that changed the invoice, `InvoiceAlreadyPaid` when another I2 finalized it,
  /// `InvalidArgument` for a withdrawn or expired invoice, and `PaymentExpired` past the
  /// time to live the payer set.
  ///
  /// Finalizing the same answer again returns the stored result. Fails with `NotPosted` when
  /// it was finalized but the node did not accept it; post it again with
  /// [`EpicWallet::repost_transaction`].
  Future<FinalizedTx> finalizeAndPost({required String slateJson});

  /// [`EpicWallet::finalize_and_post`] for the answer file at `path`.
  Future<FinalizedTx> finalizeFile({required String path});

  /// The fingerprint of the wallet's seed that its database stores, as lowercase hex: the
  /// value [`wallet_fingerprint`] gives for its recovery phrase on any chain, so compare it
  /// together with the chain. It identifies the wallet, never proves a phrase. It is no
  /// secret, but keep it with the wallet's other data, not in logs.
  Future<String> fingerprint();

  /// The finished payment of `tx_id`: its stored S3, or the I3 of an invoice this wallet
  /// issued, as legacy V3 slate JSON, which `epic-wallet post -i` reads. Save it as a file,
  /// or show it with `encode_ur_slate`, for a device that posts it, such as from an offline
  /// session. Reads only the wallet. Fails with `InvalidArgument` for an unknown transaction
  /// or one that is not `finalized`, `posted` or `confirmed`.
  Future<String> finishedPayment({required int txId});

  /// Forgets the pin of `origin`, as [`PeerPinView::origin`] names it, so the next native
  /// send there takes the key the listener shows. Use it once the user confirmed that the
  /// wallet at the origin changed its key. Returns whether there was a pin.
  ///
  /// Fails with `Locked` while the wallet is locked.
  bool forgetPeerPin({required String origin});

  /// Deletes what this wallet learned about every Epicbox key it has not heard from for
  /// `older_than_days` days, and returns how many keys it forgot. A forgotten key gets no
  /// notices until it is heard from again. Hosts call it on open with 180.
  Future<int> forgetPeers({required int olderThanDays});

  /// The running HTTP listener, or `None`.
  HttpListenerInfo? httpListenerStatus();

  /// Whether HTTP sends over Tor are on, as [`EpicWallet::set_http_over_tor`] set it.
  bool httpOverTor();

  /// Imports the transaction history of an epic-wallet wallet (the GUI's or the CLI's) whose
  /// folder is `source_dir`: the folder holding `wallet_data`, or `wallet_data` itself. The
  /// wallet must have been made from the same recovery phrase, for example by
  /// [`EpicWallet::import_wallet_seed`] from that folder's `wallet.seed`. Call it once after
  /// the import, before or after the restore scan; calling it again with the same folder
  /// only links what a later scan found and dates it.
  ///
  /// Nothing in `source_dir` is written, locked or moved: the database is copied into this
  /// wallet's folder, read from the copy, and the copy is removed. Imported transactions
  /// keep their dates, types, amounts, fees, slate ids, messages and kernel, and show
  /// `origin` `Imported`. Balances still come from the scan alone. A history that cannot be
  /// imported (none there, too old a wallet, a damaged or encrypted database, another
  /// recovery phrase) is reported in `status`, not as an error; the scan restores the funds.
  ///
  /// Asks the node for block times and for the kernels of unfinished transactions; a node
  /// that cannot be reached leaves those undone. Fails with `Locked` on a locked wallet and
  /// `Io` when the import folder cannot be made in the wallet's folder. A copy that cannot be
  /// written there, for example on a full disk, is reported as status `unreadable`.
  Future<LegacyImport> importLegacyHistory({required String sourceDir});

  /// [`EpicWallet::create`] from an `epic-wallet` `wallet.seed` instead of a phrase:
  /// `seed_file` is the file's text and `seed_password` the password it was written with.
  /// The wallet gets `password`, as `create` does; call [`EpicWallet::scan`] next. The file
  /// is only read; LEPIC keeps no copy of it.
  ///
  /// Fails with `WrongPassword` when `seed_password` does not decrypt the file,
  /// `InvalidArgument` when the text is not a `wallet.seed` (at most 4096 bytes), and
  /// otherwise as `create` does.
  static Future<EpicWallet> importWalletSeed({
    required LepicRuntime runtime,
    required WalletConfig config,
    required AppSecretStore store,
    required String seedFile,
    required String seedPassword,
    required String password,
  }) => RustLib.instance.api.crateApiWalletEpicWalletImportWalletSeed(
    runtime: runtime,
    config: config,
    store: store,
    seedFile: seedFile,
    seedPassword: seedPassword,
    password: password,
  );

  /// [`EpicWallet::import_wallet_seed`] with a wallet password LEPIC generates and seals
  /// under `pin`, as [`EpicWallet::create_with_pin`] does; store `sealed_password` as it
  /// says. Fails as `import_wallet_seed` and `create_with_pin` do.
  static Future<PinWallet> importWalletSeedWithPin({
    required LepicRuntime runtime,
    required WalletConfig config,
    required AppSecretStore store,
    required String seedFile,
    required String seedPassword,
    required String pin,
  }) => RustLib.instance.api.crateApiWalletEpicWalletImportWalletSeedWithPin(
    runtime: runtime,
    config: config,
    store: store,
    seedFile: seedFile,
    seedPassword: seedPassword,
    pin: pin,
  );

  /// The code this wallet showed for the slate `slate_id`, with the answers taken for it and
  /// their check codes. Reads only the store.
  ///
  /// Fails with `InPersonNotFound` when the wallet holds no such code.
  Future<InPersonCodeView> inPersonAnswers({required String slateId});

  /// Finalizes the answer numbered `answer` to the code for the slate `slate_id`, the one the
  /// user picked by its check code, and posts it to the node, as finalizing an invoice does.
  /// The code then takes no more answers.
  ///
  /// Fails with `InPersonNotFound` when there is no such code or answer, and as finalizing
  /// fails.
  Future<FinalizedTx> inPersonFinalize({
    required String slateId,
    required int answer,
  });

  /// Makes an in-person invoice for `amount` atomic units, with no payment proof, good for
  /// `ttl_blocks` blocks past the chain tip. The app chooses the TTL: LEPIC uses 30 when
  /// `None` and refuses one of 10 or fewer with `InvalidArgument`, as the payer stops paying
  /// 10 blocks before the cutoff. With `Online` the code names a reply mailbox on the wallet's relay, which [`EpicWallet::contacts_collect`] then
  /// watches until the code expires. The invoice and the code are stored.
  ///
  /// Fails with `InvalidArgument` for a zero amount or a TTL of 10 blocks or fewer, and as
  /// `issue_invoice` fails.
  Future<InPersonCode> inPersonInvoice({
    required BigInt amount,
    String? message,
    BigInt? ttlBlocks,
    required ContactRelayMode relayMode,
  });

  /// Pays a scanned in-person invoice of `amount`, the amount [`EpicWallet::in_person_read`]
  /// showed, as `pay_invoice` pays one, and seals the answer to the code. The payment and
  /// the answer are stored; the inputs stay locked until the code expires or the payment is
  /// cancelled. Post the answer with [`EpicWallet::in_person_post`], which returns the check
  /// code to show, or show the answer back.
  ///
  /// Fails as [`EpicWallet::in_person_read`] does, with `InvalidArgument` for a zero amount,
  /// and as `pay_invoice` fails.
  Future<InPersonReply> inPersonPay({
    required List<int> code,
    required BigInt amount,
    required BigInt minimumConfirmations,
    String? message,
    SendOptions? options,
    required ContactRelayMode relayMode,
  });

  /// Posts the stored answer for the slate `slate_id` to the reply mailbox its code named,
  /// from a key used for this slate only, through the wallet's connection to relays (over
  /// Tor when Epicbox over Tor is on). `relay_ok` says the user confirmed the mailbox
  /// [`EpicWallet::in_person_read`] reported. Posting again is safe.
  ///
  /// Fails with `RelayNotConfirmed` when `relay_ok` is false, `InPersonNotFound` when no
  /// answer is stored for the slate, `InPersonNoMailbox` when its code named no mailbox,
  /// `ContactsOffline` or `EpicboxOff` before connecting, and `TransportFailed` when the
  /// post failed.
  ///
  /// Returns the answer's check code, to show the user now and not before, and the format
  /// the answer went out in, always `Native`.
  Future<InPersonPosted> inPersonPost({
    required String slateId,
    required bool relayOk,
    required ContactRelayMode relayMode,
  });

  /// Reads a scanned code: raw `lepic.v1` bytes, from a byte-mode QR code or from
  /// [`in_person_qr_read`]. Nothing is stored. The fee of an invoice is estimated with the
  /// selection paying it would use. A code with a payment proof, without a reply key or a
  /// TTL, or whose reply mailbox is a loopback or unspecified host, is refused.
  ///
  /// Fails with `InvalidSlate` for a code that is not one or is refused.
  Future<InPersonRead> inPersonRead({
    required List<int> code,
    required BigInt minimumConfirmations,
    SendOptions? options,
    required ContactRelayMode relayMode,
  });

  /// Receives a scanned in-person send as `receive` receives a slate, and seals the answer to
  /// the code. The transaction and the answer are stored. Post the answer with
  /// [`EpicWallet::in_person_post`], which returns the check code to show, or show the answer
  /// back.
  ///
  /// Fails as [`EpicWallet::in_person_read`] does, and as `receive` fails.
  Future<InPersonReply> inPersonReceive({
    required List<int> code,
    String? message,
    required ContactRelayMode relayMode,
  });

  /// Makes an in-person send of `amount` atomic units, as [`EpicWallet::in_person_invoice`]
  /// makes an invoice, selecting outputs as `send` does. The send is stored and its inputs
  /// stay locked until an answer is finalized, the code expires or the send is cancelled.
  /// `ttl_blocks` is as for `in_person_invoice`: the app chooses it, LEPIC uses 30 when `None`
  /// and refuses one of 10 or fewer.
  ///
  /// Fails with `InvalidArgument` for a zero amount or a TTL of 10 blocks or fewer, and as
  /// `send` fails before it delivers.
  Future<InPersonCode> inPersonSend({
    required BigInt amount,
    required BigInt minimumConfirmations,
    String? message,
    SendOptions? options,
    BigInt? ttlBlocks,
    required ContactRelayMode relayMode,
  });

  /// Takes an answer to a code this wallet showed, scanned or pasted: the envelope's bytes,
  /// or its `BEGINEPICTX.` text as UTF-8. It is checked and stored as a collected answer is,
  /// and nothing is finalized.
  ///
  /// Fails with `InvalidSlate` for input that is not such an answer, `SealedForAnotherWallet`
  /// when it answers no open code of this wallet, and as the answer fails to check.
  Future<InPersonTaken> inPersonTake({required List<int> answer});

  /// As [`EpicWallet::in_person_take`], for the parts of an animated QR code scanned so far.
  ///
  /// Fails as [`in_person_qr_read`] does, with `UrCodeIncomplete` while parts are missing,
  /// and with `InvalidSlate` when the parts hold a code rather than an answer.
  Future<InPersonTaken> inPersonTakeQr({required List<String> parts});

  /// [`EpicWallet::issue_invoice_slate`] writing the I1 to the file at `path`. Fails with
  /// `NotDelivered` when the file cannot be written; the invoice is stored then, and
  /// `cancel_transaction` withdraws it.
  Future<IssuedInvoice> issueInvoiceFile({
    required BigInt amount,
    required String path,
    String? message,
  });

  /// Issuer side: issues an invoice for `amount`, what this wallet receives, and returns the
  /// I1 text for the payer, whose answer goes to [`EpicWallet::finalize_and_post`]. The
  /// invoice is stored as an incoming transaction waiting for payment;
  /// `cancel_transaction` withdraws it and [`EpicWallet::sweep_invoices`] expires it unpaid.
  ///
  /// `message` goes to the payer signed with the invoice, cut to its first 256 bytes on a
  /// character boundary.
  ///
  /// The I1 carries a time to live of 1,440 blocks, a day, from the node's tip, and a refresh
  /// here expires it unpaid from then on. A LEPIC payer refuses the invoice, and this wallet
  /// refuses to finalize a payment of it, from 10 blocks before then; this wallet refuses to
  /// retry a failed post of one from then on, so a payer whose node runs ahead never releases
  /// a payment this wallet posted.
  ///
  /// Fails with `InvalidArgument` for an amount of 0 and `NodeUnreachable` when the node's
  /// tip cannot be read; nothing is stored then.
  Future<Slate> issueInvoiceSlate({required BigInt amount, String? message});

  /// Where the chain holds the kernel with `excess`, the 33-byte commitment in hex as
  /// `TransactionDetails::kernel_excess` gives it, or `None` when the node knows none at or
  /// above `min_height` (any height when `None`). Asks the node and changes nothing in the
  /// wallet.
  ///
  /// Fails with `InvalidArgument` when `excess` is not a commitment, `Offline` in an offline
  /// session, and `NodeUnreachable` or another node kind when the node cannot be asked.
  Future<KernelLocation?> kernelOnChain({
    required String excess,
    BigInt? minHeight,
  });

  bool listenerRunning();

  /// Locks the wallet now, for example when the app goes to the background: stops the
  /// listener and any scan, wipes the root key and ends the calls still running, which fail
  /// with `Cancelled`; a store write under way is finished first. Every later call on this
  /// handle fails with `Locked`. The wallet directory is released as soon as the database
  /// closes, at once when nothing was running, so the wallet can be opened anew on a fresh
  /// handle right away; drop this one. An open that comes while the write still runs waits
  /// for it, up to about two seconds. Locking a locked wallet does nothing.
  ///
  /// A call that failed with `Cancelled` at a lock may still have stored its effect, since
  /// the write ran to its end: a send, resend or repost may be stored as sent or finalized,
  /// and a cancel as cancelled. After a lock, re-read the transactions on the next open
  /// instead of taking the failure as the outcome; a send stored as sent is delivered again
  /// with its resend, one finalized is posted again with [`EpicWallet::repost_transaction`].
  void lock();

  /// The confirmations an output needs to count as spendable in `summary` and to be spent by
  /// a send: the wallet's setting, 6 until `set_minimum_confirmations` changes it. Reads only
  /// the store.
  Future<BigInt> minimumConfirmations();

  /// The recovery phrase, after checking `password` against the stored vault. Attempts are
  /// counted and delayed as in [`EpicWallet::open`].
  Future<String> mnemonic({required String password});

  /// [`EpicWallet::mnemonic`] for a wallet from [`EpicWallet::create_with_pin`]: `pin` opens
  /// `sealed_password`, which is then checked against the stored vault. PIN attempts are
  /// counted and delayed as in [`EpicWallet::open_with_pin`].
  Future<String> mnemonicWithPin({
    required String pin,
    required String sealedPassword,
  });

  /// The native slates setting as [`EpicWallet::set_native_direct`] last saved it: what the
  /// wallet does from its next open.
  bool nativeDirect();

  /// Whether this session sends native slates to contacts that claim them: the setting as
  /// it was when the wallet opened.
  bool nativeDirectInUse();

  /// Whether the node client over Tor is on, as [`EpicWallet::set_node_over_tor`] set it.
  bool nodeOverTor();

  /// The tip, version, readiness and tip block time of the node `config` names, asked as the
  /// open wallet asks its own node: through the wallet's tor, with its onion routes, while
  /// node requests go over tor, and directly when they do not or for a node on this
  /// computer. Use it instead of [`node_tip`] while a wallet is open, also to probe a node
  /// that is not the wallet's; the wallet's node stays as it is. Only `node_url` and
  /// `node_api_secret` of `config` are used.
  ///
  /// Fails with `NodeUnreachable` when the tip or version cannot be had, `InvalidArgument`
  /// for a URL that is not one of a node, and `Locked` on a locked wallet.
  Future<NodeTip> nodeTipFor({required WalletConfig config});

  /// Applies a notice another install of this wallet received for a slate it does not hold,
  /// passed on from its `NoticeUnmatched` event with its `sender` and `notice`. It is taken
  /// under the rules of a notice received here: only from the recorded counterparty of its
  /// slate, `posted` only with the kernel the payment implies, and once; it raises the same
  /// events. Returns whether it was accepted; one for a slate this wallet does not hold
  /// either is dropped and raises nothing.
  ///
  /// Fails with `InvalidArgument` for a `sender` that is not an Epicbox address of this
  /// network or a `notice` that is not one.
  Future<bool> noticeApplyForwarded({
    required String sender,
    required String notice,
  });

  /// The wallet's onion address, `<56 characters>.onion`, with a key of its own from the
  /// phrase (ADR 0044): it is not the payment-proof address, and a payment proof asked for
  /// over the onion is signed with the onion's key. It is known without tor running and stays
  /// the same after a restore from the phrase.
  ///
  /// Fails with `Locked` while the wallet is locked.
  Future<String> onionAddress();

  /// Opens the wallet in `config.data_dir`. Any password that opens the vault opens it, also
  /// one shorter than `create` accepts today. Fails with `WrongPassword`, `WalletNotFound`
  /// when `store` holds no vault, `ForeignWallet` and `WalletBusy`, and with
  /// `DamagedDatabase` when the database was damaged and has just been set aside: open again
  /// to rebuild it, then `scan` without a start height, which then starts at genesis, to the
  /// end; [`EpicWallet::restore_scan_required`] says when it has.
  ///
  /// Every attempt is counted in `store` as `lepic.pin-failures`, and waits, as
  /// [`EpicWallet::open_with_pin`] describes; a right password clears the count. Attempts of
  /// one runtime run one at a time.
  static Future<EpicWallet> open({
    required LepicRuntime runtime,
    required WalletConfig config,
    required AppSecretStore store,
    required String password,
  }) => RustLib.instance.api.crateApiWalletEpicWalletOpen(
    runtime: runtime,
    config: config,
    store: store,
    password: password,
  );

  /// Decrypts a file from [`EpicWallet::seal_app_data`], or a `.wfeh` backup the app wrote
  /// before it used LEPIC, and returns its payload.
  ///
  /// Fails with `ForeignAppData` for an intact file of another wallet, `DamagedAppData` for a
  /// file of this wallet that was changed or cut short, `Unsupported` for a later format
  /// version, `InvalidArgument` for anything that is not such a file or is larger than
  /// 32 MiB, and `Locked`.
  Future<Uint8List> openAppData({required List<int> file});

  /// [`EpicWallet::open`] with the wallet password sealed in `sealed_password`, opened with
  /// `pin` and the PIN key in `store`. Every attempt is counted in `store` as
  /// `lepic.pin-failures` until a right PIN clears it. The first three attempts never wait;
  /// after the third failure each attempt first waits 30 seconds, twice as long after each
  /// further failure, up to an hour. Attempts of one runtime run one at a time.
  ///
  /// A wrong PIN gives `WrongPassword`, damaged text `InvalidArgument`, a store without the
  /// PIN key `SecretStore`; otherwise it fails as [`EpicWallet::open`] does.
  static Future<EpicWallet> openWithPin({
    required LepicRuntime runtime,
    required WalletConfig config,
    required AppSecretStore store,
    required String pin,
    required String sealedPassword,
  }) => RustLib.instance.api.crateApiWalletEpicWalletOpenWithPin(
    runtime: runtime,
    config: config,
    store: store,
    pin: pin,
    sealedPassword: sealedPassword,
  );

  /// Every output of the default account, spent ones included, ordered by commitment, from
  /// the store alone. The values of the outputs in each `category` add up to that part of
  /// `summary(refresh: false)`; refresh first for the node's view.
  Future<List<WalletOutput>> outputs();

  /// Answers an invite as `name`. The pending pairing is stored first, then the reply is
  /// posted unless `by_hand` is set or the mode is not `Online`; `reply_link` is returned
  /// either way, for "Send by hand" and for a post that failed (`posted` false).
  /// [`EpicWallet::contacts_collect`] takes the confirmation and raises `PairingConfirmed`.
  ///
  /// `pairing_class` is the class the user means to answer, from the preview: an invite of the
  /// other class is refused with `WrongPairingClass` before anything is stored.
  ///
  /// With `expect_address` set, from a pairing request's `from_address`, the confirmation
  /// must prove that address, and the request is marked answered. If this wallet's own
  /// request to the same wallet is out, the wallet with the lower key keeps its invite: when
  /// ours wins this fails `PairingCrossed`, otherwise our request is withdrawn.
  ///
  /// Fails as [`EpicWallet::pairing_preview`] does, with `AlreadyPaired` for an invite this
  /// wallet made or answered already or a live pairing with that address, `PairingInProgress`,
  /// `PairingCrossed`, `InviteDismissed` for a request dismissed here, and
  /// `InvalidContactName`.
  Future<PairingAnswer> pairingAccept({
    required String link,
    required String name,
    required bool byHand,
    required ContactRelayMode relayMode,
    required PairingClass pairingClass,
    String? expectAddress,
  });

  /// Cancels a pairing in progress: an invite stops working, a reply is forgotten. The other
  /// side is not told. Fails with `PairingNotFound` unless `contact_id` is in
  /// [`EpicWallet::pairings_pending`]; a paired contact is removed with
  /// [`EpicWallet::contact_remove`].
  Future<void> pairingCancel({required String contactId});

  /// Takes a reply or confirmation the user pasted, `epiccash://pair-reply/1/...` or
  /// `epiccash://pair-confirm/1/...`. A reply to one of this wallet's invites pairs the
  /// contact and returns the confirmation to send back by hand (`Confirm`); a confirmation
  /// finishes a pairing this wallet answered (`Paired`). Either way the words check follows.
  /// Reaches no relay unless the pairing has to tell the other side something.
  ///
  /// Fails with `InvalidPairingText` for any other text.
  Future<PairingLinkResult> pairingEnterLink({
    required String text,
    required ContactRelayMode relayMode,
  });

  /// Makes an invite to pair, valid for `expiry`, whose messages go through this wallet's
  /// relay. The pending pairing is stored and listed by [`EpicWallet::pairings_pending`];
  /// [`EpicWallet::contacts_collect`] takes the reply and raises `PairingReplied`. Nothing
  /// is sent, so this works offline.
  /// The pairing's mailboxes live on `home_relay`, `domain[:port]` reached over TLS, or with
  /// `None` on the wallet's own Epicbox relay; the other side reaches them there whatever
  /// relay it uses.
  ///
  /// `pairing_class` `Private` makes a private pairing with another wallet of the user's
  /// ([`PairingClass`]): its invite must travel by QR or paste.
  ///
  /// With `expect_address` set, the reply must prove that Epicbox address, compared by key:
  /// a reply without the proof fails `AddressProofMissing`, one proving another key
  /// `OtherAddress`, with nothing stored, as `PairingFailed` in the collect or as the error
  /// of `pairing_enter_link`. A reply that proves it completes the pairing Verified.
  ///
  /// Fails with `InvalidArgument` for a home relay that is not `domain[:port]`
  /// or an `expect_address` that is not an Epicbox address of
  /// this network, with `InvalidContactName` for a `name` a card cannot carry, and with `Io`
  /// when 64 pairings are pending.
  ///
  /// `kind` is deprecated and ignored: a new pairing writes a person (docs/adr/0041).
  Future<PairingInvite> pairingInvite({
    required String name,
    required ContactKind kind,
    required InviteExpiry expiry,
    required ContactRelayMode relayMode,
    String? homeRelay,
    required PairingClass pairingClass,
    String? expectAddress,
  });

  /// Reads an invite link for the "Pair with Anna?" sheet. Nothing is stored or sent.
  ///
  /// Fails with `InvalidPairingText` for text that is not an invite on the wallet's network,
  /// and `InviteExpired` for one past its expiry.
  Future<InvitePreview> pairingPreview({required String link});

  /// Hides a request. Its invite id is remembered until the invite expires, so a copy of it
  /// is dropped; a newer request from the same wallet shows again. Fails with
  /// `PairingNotFound` for no live request with that id.
  Future<void> pairingRequestDismiss({required String requestId});

  /// Asks the wallet at `to_address` to pair: a new invite of its own, good for seven days,
  /// which only that wallet can answer, posted to its payments address. The invite is kept
  /// only when the post went through; answered, the pairing completes Verified.
  ///
  /// Fails with `PeerNotProven` unless that key ran LEPIC lately, answered nothing unmarked
  /// since and claimed pair requests; `AlreadyPaired` for a live pairing with it;
  /// `PairingInProgress` for a pairing in progress or waiting with it, or a live request from
  /// it; `ContactsOffline`, `EpicboxOff`; `TransportFailed` when the post failed, with
  /// nothing kept; and `InvalidArgument` for an address of another network.
  Future<PendingPairingView> pairingRequestSend({
    required String toAddress,
    required String name,
    required ContactRelayMode relayMode,
  });

  /// The live pairing requests other wallets sent, oldest first: not dismissed, answered or
  /// expired. Answer one with [`EpicWallet::pairing_accept`], its `invite_link` and
  /// `from_address` as `expect_address`.
  Future<List<PairRequestView>> pairingRequests();

  /// Records the words check: `Match` makes the contact verified, `Mismatch` removes the
  /// pairing on this side and, when this wallet made the invite, tells the other side when
  /// the mode allows, `Skip` leaves it paired.
  ///
  /// Fails with `ContactNotFound` when no paired contact has that id.
  Future<void> pairingSas({
    required String contactId,
    required SasDecision decision,
    required ContactRelayMode relayMode,
  });

  /// The standing invite (one per wallet, seven days, the wallet's own relay), made anew when
  /// there is none, when it was answered, when less than a day is left, when its relay is not
  /// the wallet's relay now, or when `name` differs from the name it carries; on a name change
  /// the old one is withdrawn. Sends nothing, so it works offline. The link is returned again
  /// on every call; never store it.
  ///
  /// Fails with `InvalidContactName`, and `Io` when 64 pairings are pending.
  Future<PairingInvite> pairingStandingInvite({required String name});

  /// The paired contacts attached to the address-book entry `app_contact` with
  /// [`EpicWallet::contact_attach`], oldest first: usually one, two while a key check
  /// between an old and a new pairing runs.
  Future<List<ContactView>> pairingsForAppContact({required String appContact});

  /// The pairings in progress, oldest first: invites waiting for a reply and replies waiting
  /// for the confirmation, for Contacts › Pending.
  Future<List<PendingPairingView>> pairingsPending();

  /// [`EpicWallet::pay_invoice_slate`] for the I1 file at `path`. The I2 is also written to
  /// `<path>.response`, as `epic-wallet` names it. Fails with `TransportFailed` when the file
  /// cannot be read, or when the response cannot be written; the payment is stored then, its
  /// I2 is in `contact_plain_fallback(tx_id)` and `cancel_transaction` gives it up.
  Future<PaidInvoiceSlate> payInvoiceFile({
    required String path,
    required BigInt amount,
    required BigInt minimumConfirmations,
    String? message,
    SendOptions? options,
  });

  /// Payer side: pays the invoice in the I1 text `slate_json` and returns the I2 for the
  /// issuer. Call it only after the user approved `amount`, which must be the invoiced amount;
  /// the fee comes on top. `minimum_confirmations`, `message` and `options` are as in
  /// `create_send_slate`. The inputs stay locked until the issuer posts the transaction or
  /// the payment is cancelled with `cancel_transaction`.
  ///
  /// An I1 from LEPIC may carry a reply key: `sealed_answer_to_marker` with the I1 and the
  /// returned I2 seals the answer to it.
  ///
  /// Fails with `InvalidSlate` for text that is not an I1 or whose message signature does not
  /// verify, `AmountMismatch`, `InvoiceAlreadyPaid` for an invoice this wallet paid before,
  /// `PaymentExpired` from 10 blocks before the invoice's time to live,
  /// `InvoiceNotForThisChain` when the invoice was made for another network or at a wrong
  /// height, `InvoiceLockedAboveTip`, `NotEnoughFunds` and `NodeUnreachable`. Nothing is
  /// stored then. A payment the issuer never finalizes, for example the second payment of one
  /// request, expires 10 blocks after the chain reaches the invoice's time to live, and a
  /// refresh then releases its inputs unless the node holds the transaction.
  Future<PaidInvoiceSlate> payInvoiceSlate({
    required String slateJson,
    required BigInt amount,
    required BigInt minimumConfirmations,
    String? message,
    SendOptions? options,
  });

  /// What this wallet learned about the software at the Epicbox address `address` (its key
  /// alone counts) from that key's own messages, and what follows from it now. Reads only the
  /// store. Fails with `InvalidArgument` for an address that does not parse or is on the other
  /// network.
  Future<PeerCompat> peerCompat({required String address});

  /// Reports what the app learned of one of its own features with the wallet at `address`:
  /// `bit` is one host bit (16, device forwarding, or 32, the name service); `worked` says
  /// whether it worked. A bit the key never claimed does not start working. Sends nothing.
  /// Fails with `InvalidArgument` for another bit or an address that does not parse.
  Future<void> peerFeatureResult({
    required String address,
    required int bit,
    required bool worked,
  });

  /// The pins of the wallet's listeners kept by native sends over HTTP and Tor, by origin.
  ///
  /// Fails with `Locked` while the wallet is locked.
  List<PeerPinView> peerPins();

  /// The onion LEPIC 0.3.1 and earlier published: the payment-proof address in onion form,
  /// which `epic-wallet address` prints too. [`EpicWallet::start_tor`] publishes it as well
  /// while `HostPolicy::publish_previous_onion` allows.
  ///
  /// Fails with `Locked` while the wallet is locked.
  Future<String> previousOnionAddress();

  /// The private pairings (two wallets of one owner), oldest first, in progress or paired;
  /// `state` tells which. They are never listed by [`EpicWallet::contacts`] or
  /// [`EpicWallet::pairings_pending`] and never exported. Remove one with
  /// [`EpicWallet::contact_remove`] or [`EpicWallet::contact_unpair`].
  Future<List<ContactView>> privatePairings();

  /// [`EpicWallet::receive_slate`] for the slate file at `path`. The answer is also written to
  /// `<path>.response`, as `epic-wallet` names it, for a repeated slate too.
  Future<ReceivedSlate> receiveFile({required String path});

  /// Recipient side: signs an incoming send and returns the answer for the sender.
  ///
  /// The same slate again, from text, a file or the listener, returns the answer stored the
  /// first time with `repeated` set and the first call's `tx_id`; nothing is stored or
  /// signed again, so a lost answer can be handed out again. Fails with `InvalidSlate` for
  /// another slate under an id received before, or when that receive was cancelled or
  /// expired.
  Future<ReceivedSlate> receiveSlate({required String slateJson});

  /// `summary(refresh: true)` as a stream: a first event at 0 percent, then one with
  /// `summary` set at 100. A failure ends the stream with its [`WalletError`] as the stream's
  /// error, after the first event.
  Stream<RefreshProgress> refresh();

  /// Tells the wallet's tor that the app is back: call it when the app returns to the
  /// foreground and, with `network_changed`, when the device's network changed (Wi-Fi to
  /// mobile data, or back online). Tor leaves its dormant state, and after a network change
  /// it drops every connection, whose routes may be gone, builds new ones and forgets the
  /// onion services that failed while the network was gone: sends over Tor in progress then
  /// fail, and the onion stays this wallet's and is republished by tor
  /// itself, so `Ready` is not reported again. A bootstrap in progress gets its full time
  /// again. Returns once tor has taken the commands, in at most about 15 seconds.
  ///
  /// Fails with `Locked` while the wallet is locked and with `TorNotRunning` when no tor
  /// runs: start it with [`EpicWallet::start_tor`]. A tor that does not answer is ended:
  /// the call fails with `TorNotRunning` and the stream of `start_tor` gets `Failed` with
  /// `Stopped` (or `Start` before the bootstrap), then `Stopped`. While tor is still being
  /// started there is nothing to refresh and the call succeeds.
  Future<void> refreshTor({required bool networkChanged});

  /// Releases a finalized or posted send the network never took, once
  /// `TransactionDetails.releasable` says so: the send is cancelled, its inputs are unlocked
  /// and its change is dropped. The node is asked once more first.
  ///
  /// If another node still holds the transaction and mines it later, the inputs are spent by
  /// it after all, and a new send that spends them fails. A refresh within 10080 blocks of
  /// the release then confirms the send, with a transaction event, and restores its change.
  ///
  /// Fails with `NotReleasable`, with `OnChain` or `InPool` when the node's chain or pool
  /// holds the transaction, and with `NodeUnreachable` when the node cannot be asked.
  Future<void> releaseTransaction({required int txId});

  /// Posts the stored final transaction of `tx_id` to the node again.
  Future<void> repostTransaction({required int txId});

  /// Delivers the slate of send `tx_id` again over Epicbox, to the address it was sent to,
  /// after [`EpicWallet::send_epicbox`] failed with `TransportFailed` or
  /// [`TransactionDetails::undelivered`] says no attempt got through. The listener or a
  /// drain finalizes and posts it when the answer arrives; a wallet that answered the slate
  /// before answers it again the same way. Fails with `InvalidArgument` when `tx_id` is not
  /// a send over Epicbox waiting for its answer, and with `TransportFailed` when the relay fails
  /// again. Returns the format the slate went out in this time.
  Future<SlateWire> resendEpicbox({required int txId});

  /// Delivers the slate of send `tx_id` again, to the wallet listening at `url`, which may
  /// be another address or method than the first attempt, then finalizes and posts as
  /// [`EpicWallet::send_http`] does. Use it after a delivery failed. A wallet that answered
  /// the slate before answers it again the same way. The dialect is chosen again, as
  /// [`EpicWallet::send_http`] chooses it.
  ///
  /// Fails with `HttpUrlInvalid` or `PlainHttpNotAllowed` for the URL, `InvalidArgument`
  /// when `tx_id` is not a send waiting for its answer, and otherwise as
  /// [`EpicWallet::send_http`].
  Future<HttpResentTx> resendHttp({
    required int txId,
    required String url,
    required bool allowPlainHttp,
  });

  /// As [`EpicWallet::resend_http`]; `legacy` as in [`EpicWallet::send_http_dialect`].
  Future<HttpResentTx> resendHttpDialect({
    required int txId,
    required String url,
    required bool allowPlainHttp,
    required bool legacy,
  });

  /// Delivers the slate of send `tx_id` again, to the wallet at `onion`, which may be
  /// another address or method than the first attempt, then finalizes and posts as
  /// [`EpicWallet::send_tor`] does.
  ///
  /// Fails with `OnionAddressInvalid` or `TorNotRunning` before anything is sent,
  /// `InvalidArgument` when `tx_id` is not a send waiting for its answer, and otherwise as
  /// [`EpicWallet::send_tor`].
  Future<HttpResentTx> resendTor({required int txId, required String onion});

  /// As [`EpicWallet::resend_tor`]; `legacy` as in [`EpicWallet::send_http_dialect`].
  Future<HttpResentTx> resendTorDialect({
    required int txId,
    required String onion,
    required bool legacy,
  });

  /// Whether the wallet's database was rebuilt from its seed, by a restore from the phrase or
  /// a `wallet.seed`, or by an open after a damaged or missing database, and no restore scan
  /// has completed since: one from genesis, or for a restore with `restore_from_height` one
  /// from that height or below. While it is, the wallet refuses every new output: receiving,
  /// the change of a send and invoices fail with `RestoreScanRequired`. A [`EpicWallet::scan`]
  /// without a start height starts where the restore scan does while it is, and ends it when
  /// it completes.
  Future<bool> restoreScanRequired();

  /// Deletes the plain copies of the database kept in the data directory after the move to
  /// encryption: the `wallet.sqlite3.pre-encryption` file the encrypted database was copied
  /// from, which it records, and every `wallet.sqlite3.after-rollback-<unix>` file older than
  /// `older_than_days`, each with its log files. A `.pre-encryption` file it does not record
  /// is kept and listed in `kept`. Nothing else. For the release after the one that encrypts; the app decides
  /// when. Deletes nothing while the session runs on the plain database. Fails with `Locked`
  /// and `Io`.
  Future<RetiredPlainStore> retirePlainStore({required int olderThanDays});

  /// Scans the chain for this wallet's outputs from `start_height`, or, by default, from
  /// shortly before the last completed scan, streaming progress until an event with
  /// `finished`, which carries its `result`. A failure ends the stream with its
  /// [`WalletError`] as the stream's error; a cancelled scan ends it with `Cancelled`.
  ///
  /// While [`EpicWallet::restore_scan_required`] is true, a scan without `start_height`
  /// starts where the restore scan does, at genesis or at the `restore_from_height` the
  /// wallet was created with, so it resumes a restore scan that stopped, and once it
  /// completes the wallet creates outputs again. `Some(0)` scans from genesis and ends any
  /// restore scan the same way, and so does a `start_height` at or below the restore's start.
  /// A scan from a higher `start_height` finds the outputs above it but leaves the restore
  /// scan pending: run one without `start_height` after it. A `start_height` above the chain
  /// tip scans
  /// nothing: the last event's message says the scan was skipped, and its `result` has a
  /// `start_height` above its `end_height`.
  ///
  /// With `delete_unconfirmed` the scan also runs the repair of `epic-wallet scan
  /// --delete_unconfirmed` and always starts from the beginning of the chain, so
  /// `start_height` must be `None`. Every output locked by a transaction in progress that is
  /// found unspent on chain is unlocked, and every output not on chain yet is deleted; the
  /// transactions they belong to are cancelled as `cancel_transaction` cancels them. That
  /// includes sends waiting for an answer and payments signed but not yet confirmed: a
  /// payment already on its way may then fail. If one is still mined, its output is not lost,
  /// but it stays hidden until the next scan finds it. The repair covers only the outputs
  /// that were locked or unconfirmed when the scan started; sends and receives made while it
  /// runs are left alone.
  ///
  /// Every page of outputs is stored as it is scanned, so a cancelled or failed scan leaves
  /// the wallet consistent, with what it found so far stored. The next scan with the same
  /// start and the same `delete_unconfirmed` resumes after the last stored page; the repair
  /// of unconfirmed outputs runs only when a scan completes.
  ///
  /// Fails with `InvalidArgument` for `delete_unconfirmed` with a `start_height`, and with
  /// `WalletBusy` while another scan of this wallet runs.
  Stream<ScanProgress> scan({
    BigInt? startHeight,
    required bool deleteUnconfirmed,
  });

  /// Encrypts `payload` as a `WFEH` version 1 file, the format of the app's `.wfeh` history
  /// backups. Every call draws a fresh salt and nonce, so two files of one payload differ.
  /// Only this wallet, or one restored from the same recovery phrase, opens the file.
  ///
  /// Fails with `Locked`, and with `InvalidArgument` for a payload above 32 MiB.
  Future<Uint8List> sealAppData({required List<int> payload});

  /// The answer `reply` (the S2 or I2 JSON this wallet answered the plain slate `incoming`
  /// with) sealed to the reply key `incoming` carries when it came from LEPIC, or `None`
  /// when it carries none and the answer goes plain.
  Future<String?> sealedAnswerToMarker({
    required String incoming,
    required String reply,
  });

  /// Finalizes and posts the S2 or I2 in sealed text, as `finalize_slate` does.
  ///
  /// Fails as [`EpicWallet::sealed_open`] does, with `InvalidSlate` for another stage, and as
  /// `finalize_slate` does.
  Future<FinalizedTx> sealedFinalize({required String text});

  /// Opens sealed text from a file or a paste, to show what it holds before the user acts on
  /// it with [`EpicWallet::sealed_receive`], [`EpicWallet::sealed_pay`] or
  /// [`EpicWallet::sealed_finalize`] by its `stage`. Nothing is stored, except that a newer
  /// card of the contact in it refreshes the contact.
  ///
  /// Fails with `InvalidPairingText` for text that is not sealed or is for the other network,
  /// `SealedForAnotherWallet`, `SealedUnopenable`, and `InvalidSlate` when it holds no slate.
  Future<SealedSlateView> sealedOpen({required String text});

  /// Pays the I1 in sealed text; call it only after the user approved `amount`, which must
  /// be the invoiced amount. `minimum_confirmations`, `message` and `options` are as in
  /// `create_send_slate`. Returns the answer as [`EpicWallet::sealed_receive`] does.
  ///
  /// Fails as [`EpicWallet::sealed_open`] does, with `InvalidSlate` for another stage,
  /// `AmountMismatch`, `PaymentExpired`, `NotEnoughFunds`, `InvoiceAlreadyPaid`,
  /// `InvoiceLockedAboveTip` and `NodeUnreachable`.
  Future<SealedAnswer> sealedPay({
    required String text,
    required BigInt amount,
    required BigInt minimumConfirmations,
    String? message,
    SendOptions? options,
  });

  /// Receives the S1 in sealed text, as `receive_slate` does, and returns the answer sealed
  /// to the sender when it can be, else plain; the app sends it back the way the slate came.
  /// `message` is signed into the answer.
  ///
  /// Fails as [`EpicWallet::sealed_open`] does, with `InvalidSlate` for another stage, and as
  /// `receive_slate` does.
  Future<SealedAnswer> sealedReceive({required String text, String? message});

  /// Sends to `<address>@<relay>[:port]` over Epicbox. The listener or a drain finalizes and
  /// posts it when the answer arrives. Fails with `InvalidArgument`, storing nothing, for an
  /// address that [`validate_epicbox_address`] refuses, such as a key without `@<relay>`.
  /// `message` and `options` work as in [`EpicWallet::create_send_slate`].
  Future<SentTx> sendEpicbox({
    required BigInt amount,
    required BigInt minimumConfirmations,
    required String address,
    String? message,
    SendOptions? options,
  });

  /// [`EpicWallet::create_send_slate`] writing the slate to the file at `path`.
  Future<SentTx> sendFile({
    required BigInt amount,
    required BigInt minimumConfirmations,
    required String path,
    String? message,
    SendOptions? options,
  });

  /// Sends `amount` to the wallet listening at `url`, `http(s)://host[:port][/path]`, then
  /// finalizes its answer and posts the transaction. A missing port is 3415 for `http` and
  /// 443 for `https`. `message` is sent as [`EpicWallet::create_send_slate`] sends it.
  ///
  /// Plain `http` to any host but this device needs `allow_plain_http`, set only after the
  /// user agreed that the slate travels unencrypted.
  ///
  /// While the wallet's tor runs or is expected (`WalletConfig::tor_wanted`,
  /// [`EpicWallet::expect_tor`]) and HTTP sends over Tor are on
  /// ([`EpicWallet::set_http_over_tor`]), the connection goes through tor, which resolves the host,
  /// and waits for a tor that is still starting within the request timeout; a host on this
  /// device stays direct. A tor that fails fails the send: it never goes directly instead.
  /// Otherwise the wallet is reached directly.
  ///
  /// While native slates are on ([`EpicWallet::native_direct_in_use`]), a wallet that
  /// advertises a native endpoint (ADR 0052) gets the slate sealed to its key there, and the
  /// key is pinned for the URL's origin once it answered; [`EpicWallet::peer_pins`] lists the
  /// pins. Every other wallet gets the slate as every Epic wallet takes it.
  /// [`HttpSentTx::dialect`] says which. A native post never goes to this device. Use
  /// [`EpicWallet::send_http_dialect`] to ask for the legacy dialect.
  ///
  /// Fails, storing nothing, with `HttpUrlInvalid` or `PlainHttpNotAllowed` for the URL and
  /// the errors of [`EpicWallet::estimate_fee`]. For an origin with a pin it fails, storing
  /// nothing, with `PinnedNoAdvert` when the wallet there advertises no native endpoint,
  /// `PinChanged` when it advertises another key, and `PeerProbeFailed` when it could not
  /// be asked; [`WalletError::origin`] names the origin. A wallet advertising a native
  /// endpoint at `localhost` or an address of this device fails, storing nothing, with
  /// `ThisComputer` and the origin; ask for the legacy dialect. A native exchange that fails
  /// after the send was stored fails with `ThisComputer` for a name that resolves to this
  /// device and `NativeExchangeFailed` otherwise; [`WalletError::tx_id`] names the send,
  /// which waits for a resend or a cancel. Fails with `TransportFailed` when the other
  /// wallet could not be reached, refused, or answered with something else than a slate: the
  /// send is then stored as sent with its inputs locked, so deliver it again with
  /// [`EpicWallet::resend_http`] or cancel it with [`EpicWallet::cancel_transaction`]. Fails
  /// with the errors of [`EpicWallet::finalize_and_post`] except `NotPosted` when the answer
  /// could not be finalized; the send then stays sent, and a resend gets the same answer
  /// again.
  Future<HttpSentTx> sendHttp({
    required BigInt amount,
    required BigInt minimumConfirmations,
    required String url,
    required bool allowPlainHttp,
    String? message,
    SendOptions? options,
  });

  /// As [`EpicWallet::send_http`]; `legacy` sends the slate as every Epic wallet takes it
  /// whatever the other wallet advertises, with no pin read or kept.
  Future<HttpSentTx> sendHttpDialect({
    required BigInt amount,
    required BigInt minimumConfirmations,
    required String url,
    required bool allowPlainHttp,
    String? message,
    SendOptions? options,
    required bool legacy,
  });

  /// Spends the outputs `request.inputs` chooses among those with `minimum_confirmations`
  /// into the outputs `request.outputs` asks for: a normal network transaction to the wallet
  /// itself, confirmed as any send, never through its Epicbox address. The inputs are locked
  /// until it confirms. It shows as two transactions over transport `self`, a send holding
  /// the outputs after the first and a receive holding the first; the fee is that of
  /// [`EpicWallet::estimate_send_to_self`] at the same tip. With `Named` it locks nothing
  /// else: use it to make a cancelled payment final, since the other wallet may still hold a
  /// signed copy that spends its inputs, for example the issuer of an invoice that was paid,
  /// until they are spent elsewhere.
  ///
  /// Fails, storing nothing, with `InvalidArgument` when the outputs, the remainder of
  /// `Amounts` included, are not 1 to 10, an amount is 0, `Smallest` comes with `Count`,
  /// `named` is set without `Named`, `count` with `Amounts` or `amounts` with `Count`, or
  /// `Named` names no output, or one that is unknown, not unspent with
  /// `minimum_confirmations`, or named twice; with `NothingToConsolidate` when `All` or
  /// `Smallest` would spend one output into one, or `All` with `Count` 1 finds fewer than two
  /// outputs to merge; with `NotEnoughFunds` when the inputs cannot pay the fee, the amounts
  /// and one base unit into each other output; and otherwise as
  /// [`EpicWallet::estimate_fee`]. Fails with `NotPosted` when it was stored but the node
  /// did not take it: post the send again with [`EpicWallet::repost_transaction`] or cancel
  /// it with [`EpicWallet::cancel_transaction`].
  Future<SelfSentTx> sendToSelf({
    required SelfSendRequest request,
    required BigInt minimumConfirmations,
  });

  /// Sends `amount` to the wallet at `onion` over the wallet's tor, then finalizes its
  /// answer and posts the transaction, as [`EpicWallet::send_http`] does. `onion` is read as
  /// [`validate_onion_address`] reads it; the other wallet is always reached at port 80.
  /// Emits `Sending` on the tor stream once the wallet has checked it can pay; an onion that
  /// is not online takes up to about 150 seconds to fail.
  ///
  /// Fails, storing nothing, with `OnionAddressInvalid` for the address, `TorNotRunning`
  /// when tor has not bootstrapped, and the errors of [`EpicWallet::estimate_fee`]. Fails
  /// with `OnionUnreachable` when the other wallet is not online or could not be reached in
  /// time, and with `TransportFailed` when it refused or the exchange broke: the send is then
  /// stored as sent with its inputs locked, so deliver it again with
  /// [`EpicWallet::resend_tor`] or [`EpicWallet::resend_http`], or cancel it. A tor that
  /// stops during the send fails it the same way with `TorNotRunning`. Fails as
  /// [`EpicWallet::send_http`] does when the answer could not be finalized.
  ///
  /// The dialect is chosen, and pins are read and kept for the onion, as
  /// [`EpicWallet::send_http`] does, with the same errors; use
  /// [`EpicWallet::send_tor_dialect`] to ask for the legacy dialect.
  ///
  /// `options` chooses the inputs and change, `Smallest` with one change output when `None`;
  /// change outputs outside 1..=10 fail with `InvalidArgument`.
  Future<HttpSentTx> sendTor({
    required BigInt amount,
    required BigInt minimumConfirmations,
    required String onion,
    String? message,
    SendOptions? options,
  });

  /// As [`EpicWallet::send_tor`]; `legacy` as in [`EpicWallet::send_http_dialect`].
  Future<HttpSentTx> sendTorDialect({
    required BigInt amount,
    required BigInt minimumConfirmations,
    required String onion,
    String? message,
    SendOptions? options,
    required bool legacy,
  });

  /// Turns on or off, for the listener `socket`, taking payments sealed to the wallet's epic1
  /// addresses at its native endpoint. It is off for both sockets until this turns it on:
  /// the listener then answers such a payment as one sealed to a key it does not hold, so
  /// whoever has one of the wallet's epic1 addresses cannot tell that the listener is the same
  /// wallet. The setting is kept in the wallet's directory and takes effect when the listener
  /// next starts. It matters only with native slates on ([`EpicWallet::set_native_direct`]).
  /// Deleting the wallet removes it.
  ///
  /// Fails with `Io` when the setting cannot be written.
  void setEpic1AtListener({
    required ListenerSocket socket,
    required bool enabled,
  });

  /// Turns Epicbox over Tor on or off (ADR 0021); it is on when the wallet opens. While it is
  /// on and the wallet's tor runs, the connection to the wallet's Epicbox relay goes through
  /// tor: to the relay's onion when one is known, else to its domain through the Tor network.
  /// It then never goes directly: it waits for tor to connect while it starts, and for the
  /// app to start it again when it ended on its own. With tor stopped by
  /// [`EpicWallet::stop_tor`] or `lock`, or the setting off, the relay is reached directly.
  /// Addresses do not change. A listener
  /// connection that would now go another way is replaced as soon as the messages in flight
  /// on it are acknowledged, within 30 seconds; messages not acknowledged by then stay with
  /// the relay and come again. A send in progress finishes on its own connection.
  void setEpicboxOverTor({required bool enabled});

  /// Turns HTTP sends over Tor on or off; it is on when the wallet opens. While it is on and
  /// the wallet's tor runs or is expected, [`EpicWallet::send_http`] and
  /// [`EpicWallet::resend_http`] go through tor, as they describe. Off, they reach the other
  /// wallet directly even while tor runs, for an app that runs tor only to be paid at its
  /// onion and to send to onions. Sends to onions ([`EpicWallet::send_tor`]) always go
  /// through tor. A send in progress finishes on its own connection.
  void setHttpOverTor({required bool enabled});

  /// Sets the wallet's minimum confirmations, 1 to 30 (anything else fails with
  /// `InvalidArgument`), and re-splits the balance at once without the node: the next
  /// `summary(refresh: false)` and the balance events use it. Pass this value as the
  /// `minimum_confirmations` of a send so it spends what the balance calls spendable.
  Future<void> setMinimumConfirmations({required BigInt count});

  /// Turns native slates to contacts (RFC 0003) on or off for this wallet. While it is on,
  /// new slates to a contact that claims native slates over a pairing that is not private
  /// go as native slates; [`ContactView`] says so in `native_slates`. It is off until this
  /// turns it on. The setting is kept in the wallet's directory and takes effect when the
  /// wallet next opens; [`EpicWallet::native_direct_in_use`] says what this session does.
  /// Deleting the wallet removes it.
  ///
  /// Fails with `Io` when the setting cannot be written.
  void setNativeDirect({required bool enabled});

  /// Says that [`EpicWallet::start_tor`] follows, so that a relay connection made before it,
  /// such as the listener's when the wallet opens, waits for tor instead of going directly.
  /// Call it before [`EpicWallet::start_listener`] when tor is about to start and Epicbox over
  /// Tor is on. Until `start_tor`, relay connections wait. To restart tor, call it before
  /// [`EpicWallet::stop_tor`]: that stop keeps the relay connection waiting for the
  /// `start_tor` that follows. A further `stop_tor`, or `lock`, ends the wait. Nothing on iOS.
  ///
  /// Fails with `Locked` while the wallet is locked.
  /// Turns the node client over Tor on or off; it is on when the wallet opens. While it is
  /// on and the wallet's tor runs or is expected (see [`EpicWallet::expect_tor`]), every
  /// request to the node goes through tor: to the node's onion when one is known, else to
  /// its domain, which tor resolves, through the Tor network. The system resolver is not
  /// used and nothing goes directly; while tor starts, requests wait for it, within their
  /// timeouts, and fail with `NodeUnreachable` if it does not connect. The node's TLS
  /// certificate is checked for its domain either way. With tor stopped by
  /// [`EpicWallet::stop_tor`] or `lock`, or the setting off, the node is reached directly.
  /// A request in progress finishes on its own connection.
  void setNodeOverTor({required bool enabled});

  /// Replaces the onion routes the wallet uses through tor, for example with the signed list
  /// the app fetched; until then the wallet uses the `onion_routes` of its `WalletConfig`,
  /// for the relay and for the node client. LEPIC has none of its own. Only TLS hosts belong
  /// here: the connection still checks `host`'s certificate. Applies from the
  /// next connection. An empty list leaves no route, so every host goes through a Tor exit.
  ///
  /// Fails with `InvalidArgument`, changing nothing, when a host is not a host name, an onion
  /// is not an onion v3 address, a port is 0, or there are more than 256 routes.
  void setOnionRoutes({required List<OnionRoute> routes});

  /// Moves the open wallet to the node and Epicbox relay `config` names, keeping it unlocked.
  /// Calls running when it starts end first, and calls made while it waits or runs start
  /// after it. A running scan is cancelled and ends with `Cancelled`, so the switch never
  /// waits minutes for it; scan again after the switch. A running listener moves to a new
  /// relay once the message in hand is settled and acknowledged, at most 30 seconds later,
  /// and its stream goes on without `Stopped`. A new relay changes the wallet's Epicbox
  /// address to `<key>@<new relay>`; answers sent to the old address stay on the old relay.
  ///
  /// `config.data_dir` and `config.chain` must be those the wallet was opened with: another
  /// chain is another wallet. Fails with `InvalidArgument`, keeping the servers in use, for
  /// a node URL or relay that is not one, for another directory or chain, and when
  /// `config` turns Epicbox on or off; with `Locked` on a locked wallet.
  Future<void> setServers({required WalletConfig config});

  /// Signs `message` for the app's `domain` with the key of the listing address of
  /// [`EpicWallet::epicbox_address_at`], and returns the DER signature in hex, which
  /// [`verify_address_signature_at`] checks. `domain` is lowercase `name/version`, such as
  /// `epic-listing/1`, at most 64 bytes; domains starting with `lepic`, in any case, are
  /// refused. The digest is tagged so that no other signature of the wallet can equal it.
  /// Needs no relay. Fails with `InvalidArgument` for another `service_key` or `domain` and for a
  /// `message` over 64 KiB.
  Future<String> signWithAddressKeyAt({
    required String serviceKey,
    required int generation,
    required String domain,
    required List<int> message,
  });

  /// Starts the HTTP listener on `port` and streams what it does until
  /// [`EpicWallet::stop_http_listener`], [`EpicWallet::lock`] or the wallet is dropped. Port
  /// 0 lets the system pick one, which the `Started` event and
  /// [`EpicWallet::http_listener_status`] report; `epic-wallet` listens on 3415.
  ///
  /// With `coinbase` the listener also answers a mining node's `build_coinbase` (ADR 0046,
  /// ADR 0048), so the node pays its block rewards to this wallet.
  ///
  /// The first event is `Started`, `PortInUse` or `BindFailed`; after a start the last one
  /// is `Stopped`. A wallet runs one HTTP listener: a call while one runs or starts gets a
  /// stream that ends at once. On a locked wallet the stream ends with `Locked` as its error
  /// and no event.
  Stream<HttpListenerEvent> startHttpListener({
    required HttpExposure exposure,
    required int port,
    required HttpCoinbase coinbase,
  });

  /// Starts the Epicbox listener, unless it runs already, and streams what it does until
  /// [`EpicWallet::stop_listener`]. The last event is `Stopped`; a listener that fails while
  /// running reports an `Error` event first. When the listener cannot start, for example
  /// with `Unsupported` when the configuration names no relay, the stream ends with that
  /// [`WalletError`] as its error and no event. A second call while the listener runs gets
  /// a stream that ends at once.
  Stream<ListenerEvent> startListener();

  /// Starts the wallet's tor from the binary at `tor_binary`, with `lib_dir` on the library
  /// path for the libraries the Tor Expert Bundle ships on Linux, and streams what it does
  /// until [`EpicWallet::stop_tor`], [`EpicWallet::lock`] or the wallet is dropped. Tor keeps
  /// its files in `tor` inside the wallet's data directory.
  ///
  /// The first event is `Starting`, or `Failed` with `Listener` when the wallet's listener
  /// could not start; the last one is always `Stopped`. A wallet runs one tor: a call while one
  /// runs or starts gets a stream that ends at once. On a locked wallet the stream ends with
  /// `Locked` as its error and no event. A call right after a stop or lock waits for the tor
  /// stopped last to be down, as both use the same files; when that takes over 30 seconds,
  /// the stream gets `Failed` with `Start`, then `Stopped`. A tor that an earlier run of the
  /// app left running on the wallet's files, as after the app was killed, is halted first.
  /// The onion is the same on every start. On iOS nothing is started: the stream gets
  /// `Failed` with `Missing`, then `Stopped`.
  ///
  /// With `publish_onion` false the wallet's onion is not published and no listener runs
  /// behind it: tor only carries the node, relay and Tor-send connections, and the stream
  /// stops at `Bootstrapped`, never `Publishing` or `Ready`.
  ///
  /// With `publish_onion` and `HostPolicy::publish_previous_onion` (the default), the
  /// previous onion is published too, once the onion is, behind a listener of its own. The
  /// stream reports only the onion: a previous onion that cannot be added is logged.
  Stream<TorEvent> startTor({
    required String torBinary,
    String? libDir,
    required bool publishOnion,
  });

  /// Stops the HTTP listener. Requests in progress finish; its stream ends with `Stopped`
  /// once the port is free.
  void stopHttpListener();

  /// Stops the listener. Its stream ends with `Stopped` shortly after.
  void stopListener();

  /// Stops the wallet's tor, then its listener. The stream of `start_tor` ends with
  /// `Stopped` once both are down. Sends over Tor in progress fail.
  void stopTor();

  /// How the wallet's database was found on disk when it was unlocked. Fails with `Locked`.
  Future<StoreAtRest> storeAtRest();

  /// The default account's balance. With `refresh` it first updates outputs and
  /// transactions from the node. Without, it answers from what the wallet stored at its last
  /// refresh or scan, without the node, also before the first refresh of this handle, with
  /// `validated_with_node` false.
  Future<WalletSummary> summary({required bool refresh});

  /// Expires what invoices left unpaid; run it from time to time, for example after
  /// collecting. Invoices from contacts received more than `expiry_secs` ago and still
  /// waiting are deleted, and invoices this wallet issued that long ago, to a contact or by
  /// file, and not paid are cancelled, each with an `InvoiceExpired` event. Payments of
  /// invoices whose time to live passed with the transaction neither on chain nor in the
  /// node's pool are listed in `cancellable`, unchanged.
  ///
  /// `expiry_secs` defaults to a day and may be at most two days. Fails with
  /// `InvalidArgument` for 0 or more than two days, `NodeUnreachable` when a payment past its
  /// time to live cannot be looked up, and as `cancel_transaction` does.
  Future<InvoiceSweep> sweepInvoices({BigInt? expirySecs});

  /// Tells the wallet's tor that the device has no network: call it when the device's
  /// network goes (airplane mode, no signal). Tor turns its network off at once and makes no
  /// connection attempt until the next [`EpicWallet::refresh_tor`], which turns it on again
  /// whatever its `network_changed`; attempts made without a network would mark the relays
  /// tor enters the Tor network through as unreachable, and slow its return for minutes.
  /// Sends over Tor in progress fail. Does nothing when no tor runs, while the wallet is
  /// locked, or while tor is still being started (a network turned off would stall it).
  ///
  /// A tor that does not answer is ended, as by [`EpicWallet::refresh_tor`]: the call fails
  /// with `TorNotRunning`.
  Future<void> torNetworkLost();

  TorStatus torStatus();

  /// The transaction `tx_id`, from the store alone. Fails with `InvalidArgument` when the
  /// wallet holds no such transaction.
  Future<TransactionDetails> transaction({required int txId});

  /// Every transaction of the default account, newest first, from the store alone.
  Future<List<TransactionDetails>> transactions();
}

/// A wallet created by [`EpicWallet::create_with_pin`], and its wallet password sealed under the
/// PIN and the PIN key in the app's store. Store `sealed_password`;
/// [`EpicWallet::open_with_pin`] needs it. With the store's contents it allows guessing the
/// PIN offline, so keep it where the store is, not in backups.
class PinWallet {
  final EpicWallet wallet;
  final String sealedPassword;

  const PinWallet({required this.wallet, required this.sealedPassword});

  @override
  int get hashCode => wallet.hashCode ^ sealedPassword.hashCode;

  @override
  bool operator ==(Object other) =>
      identical(this, other) ||
      other is PinWallet &&
          runtimeType == other.runtimeType &&
          wallet == other.wallet &&
          sealedPassword == other.sealedPassword;
}
